.TH EXPRESSO 1 "2026-11-21" "expresso" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
expresso \- keep the system awake for a while
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expresso on
.I DURATION
.RB [ \-\-lid ]
.RB [ \-\-idle ]
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B expresso off
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B expresso status
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B expresso
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B expresso
holds a lock that keeps the system from suspending, for a given number of
seconds or until turned off.
It is meant to be scripted: output on stdout is data only, messages go to
stderr, and every outcome has its own exit status.
.PP
A lock is a transient systemd user unit,
.BR app-expresso.service ,
running
.BR systemd-inhibit (1).
While the unit runs, the lock is held; there is no other state.
Each user has at most one lock, shared by all their sessions.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI on " DURATION"
Take the lock for
.I DURATION
seconds, or until
.B off
if
.I DURATION
is 0.
.I DURATION
is a whole number from 0 to 2147483647, without sign or leading zeros.
Fails if a lock is already on.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-lid
Also keep closing the laptop lid from suspending the system.
.TP
.B \-\-idle
Also keep idle daemons, such as
.BR hypridle ,
from dimming, locking or turning off the screen.
.TP
.B \-\-notify
If a lock is already on, also show it as a desktop notification; see
.BR NOTIFICATIONS .
The exit status is the same.
.RE
.IP
The flags can appear in any order, each at most once.
.TP
.B off
Release the lock. Returns once it is fully released.
Fails if no lock is on.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-notify
If no lock is on, also show that as a desktop notification.
The exit status is the same.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Print the lock's state; see
.BR "STATUS OUTPUT" .
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-notify
Also show the state as a desktop notification; see
.BR NOTIFICATIONS .
The output and exit status are the same.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH STATUS OUTPUT
.B status
prints
.IB key = value
lines.
When no lock is on, the only line is
.BR state=off .
When a lock is on, all of these keys are printed, in this order:
.TP
.B state
.BR on .
.TP
.B duration
The
.I DURATION
given to
.BR on ;
0 for an indefinite lock.
.TP
.B started
When the lock was taken, as a Unix timestamp.
.TP
.B until
When it ends, as a Unix timestamp, or
.B infinity
for an indefinite lock.
.TP
.B remaining
Seconds until it ends, or
.BR infinity .
.TP
.BR lid ", " idle
.B true
or
.BR false :
whether
.B \-\-lid
and
.B \-\-idle
were given.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
state=on
duration=1800
started=1759158600
until=1759160400
remaining=1740
lid=false
idle=true
.fi
.RE
.SH TIMING AND SUSPEND
The deadline is wall-clock time:
.B on 1800
means until 30 minutes from now, and time spent suspended counts.
If the system resumes after the deadline, the lock ends at once.
.PP
The lock always blocks suspend requests, such as
.B systemctl suspend
or an idle daemon's suspend.
Closing the lid still suspends the system unless
.B \-\-lid
was given, because
.BR logind.conf (5)
ignores such locks for the lid by default
.RB ( LidSwitchIgnoreInhibited=yes ).
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
The lock unit itself sends desktop notifications, whatever started or stopped
it: when the lock is taken ("Expresso on"), when it ends ("Time's up" or
"Turned off"), and when it fails ("Expresso failed", with where to look in the
journal).
.PP
.B status \-\-notify
shows the lock's state on request, for example from a keybinding: "Expresso
status: on" with when it ends and the time left ("Until 15:40 (29m left) \-
idle"), or "Expresso status: off" with "No lock is on".
With
.BR \-\-notify ,
.B on
and
.B off
also notify when there is nothing to do, since the lock unit then sends
nothing: "Expresso status: already on" with the running lock, as
.B status
shows it, or "Expresso status: already off" with "No lock is on".
Without it, a toggle such as
.B "expresso off || expresso on 3600"
shows only the lock unit's notification.
Otherwise the commands send no notifications themselves, and
.B on
and
.B off
print nothing on success.
.PP
The titles of the notifications asked for with
.B \-\-notify
say "status:", which tells them apart from the lock unit's own.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR status :
a lock is on.
.TP
.B 1
No lock is on
.RB ( status ", " off ).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown command or option, invalid or missing
.IR DURATION .
.TP
.B 3
A lock is already on
.RB ( on ).
.TP
.B 4
System error: systemd could not be reached, the lock could not be taken or
released, or
.B app-expresso.service
exists but was not started by this version of
.BR expresso ,
or a notification asked for with
.B \-\-notify
could not be sent.
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep the system awake for 45 minutes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
expresso on 2700
.fi
.RE
.PP
Until turned off, with the screen kept on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
expresso on 0 \-\-idle
.fi
.RE
.PP
Toggle, for example from a keybinding:
.PP
.RS
.nf
expresso off 2>/dev/null || expresso on 3600 \-\-idle
.fi
.RE
.PP
Separate keys to turn it on and off, each with a notification even when there
is nothing to do, and one to show the state:
.PP
.RS
.nf
expresso on 0 \-\-notify
expresso off \-\-notify
expresso status \-\-notify >/dev/null
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check for a lock, and read a value:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if expresso status >/dev/null; then
    expresso status | sed \-n 's/^remaining=//p'
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
None. There is no configuration file; put your preferred flags in a
keybinding, alias or script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-inhibit (1),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR logind.conf (5),
.BR notify-send (1)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at https://github.com/Davi-S/expresso/issues
